      <cell id="al9aepkbn">
        <level>home</level>
        <order>1000</order>
        <desc>OPTION 5.0 - &amp;quot;MODALITY-FIRST WITH SHARED CLINICAL CORE.&amp;quot; Rationale: Like Option 4.0, this still leads with the EN vs PN choice - but instead of fully duplicating every workflow stage under both therapies, it keeps each modality section LEAN (modality-specific essentials only) and consolidates cross-modality content into SHARED HUBS: &amp;quot;Monitoring &amp;amp; Complications,&amp;quot; &amp;quot;Evidence &amp;amp; Guidelines,&amp;quot; &amp;quot;Education &amp;amp; Tools,&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;Patient &amp;amp; Caregiver Resources.&amp;quot; It also promotes &amp;quot;By Clinical Condition&amp;quot; to a CO-EQUAL primary door, giving a true dual front door on the homepage (&amp;quot;I&amp;apos;m managing a therapy&amp;quot; vs &amp;quot;I&amp;apos;m treating a condition&amp;quot;). BENEFITS vs 4.0: far less content duplication and lower long-term maintenance; complications/monitoring become a single authoritative, modality-agnostic library (preserving the v3.0 insight that complications are the key differentiator, but making it serve EN and PN equally); condition pathway gets equal billing. TRADE-OFF: complications live in a shared library rather than nested inside each therapy, so this model leans more on cross-linking and tagging to keep content contextual. Audience: Registered Dietitians (RDs/RDNs) are PRIMARY (clinical, critical care, oncology, GI, pediatric, renal, home nutrition support); Nutrition Support Pharmacists are CO-PRIMARY. Because pharmacists operationalize most PN decisions, PN Safety &amp;amp; Administration and Pharmacy Compatibility &amp;amp; Stability are deliberately kept UNDER the PN section (not in the shared hub) as a pharmacist fast-path; only Monitoring and the Complications Library are shared.</desc>
        <text>Home</text>
      </cell>
